# What the SOURCE distribution carries, which is not the same question as what a wheel carries.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `setup.py` exists to make one artefact unbuildable: a platform-neutral package containing a
# Linux executable. It hooks `bdist_wheel`, so it tags a wheel that carries binaries and refuses
# to let it claim `py3-none-any`. It does not hook `sdist`, and nothing else did either.
#
# The result, measured on 2026-09-10: `python -m build --sdist` produced a tarball with 33 entries
# under `vendor/bin/linux-x86_64/`, roughly 19 MB of `.so` files and `llama-quantize`, inside a
# distribution that has no platform tag by construction. A macOS or Windows user with no matching
# wheel, or anyone installing with `--no-binary`, builds from that sdist and gets Linux binaries.
# `doctor` then reports that the vendored binary "cannot start", which is the corrupt-at-first-use
# outcome `setup.py`'s own docstring names as the thing it prevents.
#
# So the binaries are pruned here. The sdist keeps the vendored SOURCE (`vendor/src/`), which is
# what a source distribution should carry, and keeps every licence notice, because the MIT
# obligation attaches to the source as much as to the binaries.

prune src/senbonzakura/vendor/bin
recursive-include src/senbonzakura/vendor/bin LICENSE

include LICENSE
include THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
include THIRD-PARTY-CORPORA.md
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include constraints.txt
recursive-include constraints *.txt
